WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.162
Deduplication should begin with detection, not deletion.

00:00:04.162 --> 00:00:09.967
First, normalize the fields you plan to use as identity evidence.

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:18.007
For example, convert emails to a consistent form and normalize name spacing and capitalization.

00:00:18.007 --> 00:00:23.302
Second, group records using the duplicate key you have chosen.

00:00:23.302 --> 00:00:27.587
Third, review groups where the count is greater than one.

00:00:28.138 --> 00:00:30.555
At this point, do not delete anything.

00:00:30.555 --> 00:00:34.585
Two rows can look similar without representing the same person.

00:00:34.585 --> 00:00:37.375
Two family members might share a phone number.

00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:40.330
A person might use two email addresses.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.440
A common name can appear many times.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.345
The goal of the first stage is to identify candidate duplicate groups.

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:55.805
Only after you understand the relationship should you decide whether records should be merged, ranked, quarantined,

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:57.346
or simply left alone.
